digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate UNITO as an automated cytometry-gating framework that converts protein-expression measurements into bivariate density images and uses image-segmentation-style, hierarchical region prediction to reduce manual gating labor, preserve intermediate gating structure, handle pre-gating challenges, and support evaluation across flow and mass cytometry datasets.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Across cytometry workflows, manual and existing automated gating approaches face labor, hierarchy, interpretability, modality, variance, and pre-gating limitations; representing bivariate density maps as image-like objects and using hierarchical region-based prediction motivates a unified automated gating framework."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Manual boundary definition for each subtype and sample is time-consuming and labor-intensive, so automation is a plausible way to reduce labor and improve scalability in cytometry analysis."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Because experts use a global view of cell density in protein-measurement space, methods that process the whole bivariate density map as an image are suited to emulate expert gating behavior."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Because gating is implemented as a boundary or polygon on a bivariate density map, gating can be reframed as identifying regions on an image-like density map."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) CNNs have properties relevant to this problem space: they can address biological variance through translational invariance or equivariance and have been adapted to image-segmentation tasks."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Most existing dense neural-network approaches perform one-step global cell-type classification, but such terminal-only prediction is insufficient for reproducing hierarchical workflows with intermediate gates."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Because hierarchical gating guarantees interpretability of target populations, automated gating methods should preserve hierarchical interpretability."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) Because no software can configure a hierarchical series of bivariate pre-gating and gating tasks, a method should include explicit task orchestration of the gating tree."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) Because predicting only terminal cell types prevents accurate gating of intermediate cell types, hierarchical gate prediction is needed rather than only terminal classification."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) Manual pre-gating and manual boundary definition impose substantial labor and require human-chosen parameters, motivating automated approaches that reduce manual parameterization and labor."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) When gating is reframed as region identification on image-like density maps, segmentation-derived pixel-level regions can be converted into contours or polygons that define gates and can be mapped back to cell labels."];
  "R21" [label="(0,0,0) Predicted segmentation regions that can be converted to contours can serve as gating polygons on bivariate density maps."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) A method that processes the whole bivariate density map as an image is well suited to emulate the global density view used by expert manual gating."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) Because flow and mass cytometry use similar gating procedures for cell-type identification, validating a modality-agnostic gating method across both modalities would support claims of cross-modality generalization."];
  "R24" [label="(0,0,0) Representing cytometry data as a bivariate density image can support expert-like gating because it enables methods to process the global density structure used in manual gating."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Pre-gating is a recurring manual step in both standard manual analysis and typical unsupervised clustering workflows, motivating attention to pre-gating in automated cytometry analysis."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Sample-preparation and instrument variation can shift protein-detection intensity and population shape, so automated pre-gating methods should adaptively handle such between-sample shifts."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Because flow and mass cytometry lack similar strategies to detect cell aggregates, aggregate and doublet handling is an additional pre-gating challenge that motivates robust automated pre-gating."];
  "i1" [label="(0,0,0) If cytometry analysis is high-throughput and central to immunology, then automating its analysis pipeline will increase scalability and reduce labor."];
  "i13" [label="(0,0,0) If automatic pre-gating is challenged by technical/biological variance, then the method must adaptively handle shifts in intensity and population shape between samples."];
  "i15" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given that flow cytometry has spillover due to spectral overlap and mass cytometry has minimal spillover due to discrete masses, therefore mass cytometry helps avoid fluorescence compensation difficulties and enables higher-parameter measurement."];
  "i2" [label="(0,0,0) If gating is performed on bivariate density maps (polygons/contours on 2D density), then the gating task can be reframed as identifying regions on an 'image-like' density map."];
  "i21" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given that hierarchical gating improves interpretability and defines excluded out-of-boundary cells, therefore an automated gating method should preserve hierarchical interpretability and explicitly handle excluded events."];
  "i28" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: If flow and mass cytometry lack similar strategies to detect cell aggregates, then aggregate/doublet handling is an additional pre-gating challenge that motivates robust automated pre-gating."];
  "i3" [label="(0,0,0) If manual gating requires a global view of density, then methods that process the whole bivariate density map as an image are suited to emulate expert behavior."];
  "i30" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: If only predicting terminal types prevents accurate intermediate gating, then hierarchical gate prediction is needed rather than only terminal classification."];
  "i40" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: If a modality-agnostic method is desired and gating procedures are similar across modalities, then validating across both modalities supports generalization."];
  "i44" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: If software cannot configure hierarchical series of bivariate tasks, then a method must include explicit task orchestration of the gating tree."];
  "i49" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: If predicted regions can be converted to contours, then the resulting contours can serve as gating polygons on bivariate density maps."];
  "i6" [label="(0,0,0) If segmentation yields pixel-level regions, then predicted regions can be converted into contours/polygons that define gates and then mapped back to cell labels."];
  "i8" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given that predicting only terminal cell types fails for intermediate gating and manual gating is tree-structured, therefore one-step global classifiers are insufficient to reproduce hierarchical manual gating."];
  "n10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently, manual boundary definition for each subtype and sample is time-consuming and labor-intensive."];
  "n14" [label="(14,1,0) Currently, manual analysis first separates live/viable single cells from unwanted events, then gates into populations."];
  "n17" [label="(17,1,0) Currently, unsupervised clustering typically requires manual pre-gating before it can be applied."];
  "n18" [label="(18,1,0) Currently, manual pre-gating requires substantial labor, and even bivariate pipelines need human-chosen parameters."];
  "n20" [label="(20,1,0) Currently, no software can configure a hierarchical series of bivariate pre-gating (and gating) tasks."];
  "n23" [label="(23,1,0) Currently, most existing methods do one-step global cell-type classification using multi-channel dense neural networks."];
  "n26" [label="(26,2,0) Currently, sample prep and instrument variation cause fluctuations in protein detection and population shape even with fixed protocol/panel."];
  "n27" [label="(27,1,0) Currently, predicting only terminal cell types prevents accurate gating of intermediate cell types."];
  "n28" [label="(28,1,0) Currently, manual bivariate gating is tree-structured: parent populations are split into subpopulations to obtain intermediate gating results."];
  "n29" [label="(29,1,0) Currently, hierarchical gating guarantees interpretability of target populations."];
  "n33" [label="(33,2,0) Currently, convolutional neural networks (CNNs) can address biological variance using translational invariance/equivariance."];
  "n36" [label="(36,1,0) Currently, CNN versatility enabled adaptation to image segmentation."];
  "n37" [label="(37,1,0) Currently, manual gating is hard to automate because experts use a global view of cell density in protein-measurement space."];
  "n4" [label="(4,3,0) Currently, in flow cytometry fluorophore spectral overlap causes spillover between channels."];
  "n5" [label="(5,3,0) Currently, in mass cytometry discrete atomic masses enable minimal spillover."];
  "n6" [label="(6,2,0) Currently there is a gap: flow cytometry and mass cytometry do not have similar strategies to detect cell aggregates."];
  "n7" [label="(7,3,0) Currently, gating is implemented as a boundary/polygon on a bivariate (2D) density map of the cell population."];
  "n9" [label="(9,3,0) Currently, despite different technologies, flow and mass cytometry employ similar gating procedures for cell-type identification."];
  "n27" -> "i8" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "n28" -> "i8" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "n4" -> "i15" [label="deduction-case"];
  "n5" -> "i15" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "n10" -> "R1" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "i1" -> "R1" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
